<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gelimer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned 530–<a href="/years/534">534</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gelimer was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. In 530 he deposed his pro-Roman cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilderic">Hilderic</a>, giving Emperor Justinian a pretext for war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> defeated him at Ad Decimum and Tricamarum in 533, and after a winter besieged in the mountains he surrendered in 534. He was paraded in Belisarius' triumph at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and given estates in Galatia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gelimer.jpg" alt="Gelimer" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%BCnze_50_Denari_-_Gelimer_-_K%C3%B6nig_der_Vandalen_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Gelimer's early life, and his birth date is unknown. He was the son of Gelarius, grandson of Gento and great-grandson of Geiseric, who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> in the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>. He had two brothers, Ammatus and Tzazon, and became a military commander who campaigned successfully against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>. Apart from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilderic">Hilderic</a> he was the heir expected to inherit the throne. Hilderic had sought better relations with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> and ended persecutions and allowed a new Chalcedonian bishop to take office at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, which alarmed the Arian Vandal nobility. Gelimer allied with the Moors and persuaded the nobles to depose him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 19 May 530 Gelimer imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilderic">Hilderic</a> and took the throne. Justinian refused his gifts and rejected his legitimacy, and prevented an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>. Revolts in Tripolitania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> followed, and Justinian supported both. Gelimer sent his brother Tzazon with 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> to Sardinia, which deprived him of troops and nearly the whole fleet. The historian J. B. Bury called his actions astounding blunders.</p><p>In June 533 Justinian sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> with an expeditionary force. Gelimer confronted him at Ad Decimum on 13 September 533; his plan to ambush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> failed when his brother Ammatus arrived early and was killed, which set off a rout. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> was captured in the following days. On 15 December 533 the armies met again at Tricamarum, where Tzazon died and the Vandal centre collapsed. Gelimer fled to Mons Pappua, on the border of Numidia, where Pharas besieged him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In March 534 Gelimer surrendered, ending the Vandal Kingdom. He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>'s triumph, where according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> he cried out the verse from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastes">Ecclesiastes</a>, vanity of vanities. He refused to abandon his Arian faith and so did not receive the title of patrikios. Justinian gave him and his supporters estates in Galatia, where he lived until the end of his life. The year of his death is unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gelimer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gaiseric styled himself King of the Vandals and Alans and promulgated a law of succession by which the oldest male of the royal house was to succeed; it carried the crown to his son Huneric in 477, then to Huneric's cousin Gunthamund, to Thrasamund, Hilderic and <b>Gelimer</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>West of Carthage, the Byzantine army of Belisarius defeated the Vandal king <b>Gelimer</b>, whose brother Tzazon was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-15">15 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At the tenth milestone south of Carthage, the Byzantine general Belisarius defeated the Vandal army of King <b>Gelimer</b>, whose attempt to trap the invaders failed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-13">13 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Africa. Died in Galatia. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But as for me, I am not able to write further. For my present misfortune has robbed me of my thoughts. Farewell, then, dear Pharas, and send me a lyre and one loaf of bread and a sponge, I pray you.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter from the siege on Mount Papua, 533-534 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 4) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1916 &#183; the last Vandal king's answer to the besieging commander Pharas, as Procopius gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16765/pg16765-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
